hermes v0.23.0: in-flight dashboard (desktop/web chat) turns survive a
dashboard restart and continue on their own.

Every deploy that changes the agent's environment restarts
hermes-dashboard-<agent>, which runs the web/desktop chat turns and their
in-process subagents. Whether such a turn continued afterwards was a race,
and when it did, only once a client reopened the session within 15 min:

A. Shutdown deleted the restart marker. SIGTERM ->
   tui_gateway/session_reaper.py _handle_exit_flush_signal ->
   _stop_turns_before_exit interrupts every running turn exactly like a
   user Stop; the turn then concludes as "interrupted" and
   prompt_turn.py (_complete_turn_payload / run_body's finally) retires its
   crash marker (desktop/interrupted_turns.json, turn_marker.py). The
   marker survived only when the process died before the turn thread got
   there. Now the exit path flags the session (_shutdown_interrupted)
   before interrupting it, the interrupt is labelled "gateway shutdown"
   (as the messaging gateway's own), and the retire sites keep the marker
   for such a turn unless it completed (session_auto_continue.py
   _shutdown_keeps_marker). A user Stop is unchanged: session.interrupt
   retires the marker itself (methods_session_interrupt.py).

B. Nothing resumed server-side. Auto-continue ran only on session.resume
   (session_auto_continue.py _maybe_schedule_auto_continue), and saved
   subagent results only reached a session whose notification poller ran,
   i.e. an open one; a startup-replayed result even required a live
   client (session_notifications.py _session_owns_notification_event).
   New, opt-in `desktop.auto_continue.on_startup` (default false): shortly
   after the dashboard serves (web_server.py _on_server_started), it
   reopens, unattached, every session of the launch home and of every
   profile that has a marker whose writer is dead (within
   freshness_minutes, below max_attempts) or a pending subagent/background
   result (recover_abandoned_delegations first). The reopen is the normal
   session.resume (cold path), so the existing auto-continue runs with
   its existing prompt ("[System note: Your previous turn was interrupted
   mid-run ... check the current state before redoing anything ..."),
   rendered as an auto_continue row, and its attempt cap (max_attempts)
   still breaks crash loops. Only the dashboard's own chat sessions are
   reopened (stored source "desktop" or "tui", what
   agent_factory._resolve_session_source gives the Desktop/Webapp chat and
   the TUI); everything else (no stored row, a messaging platform, cron, a
   one-shot CLI run, the "tool" sidecar, a hosted room) is left to its own
   owner. Such a session is marked _server_resumed and owns its
   startup-replayed subagent results without a client. A client opening it
   later attaches to the live session. At most 16 reopened sessions
   (skipped ones do not count).

D. No drain. New `dashboard.shutdown_drain_seconds` (default 0 = off): on
   the first SIGTERM while a running turn is inside a tool call
   (agent._executing_tools), uvicorn keeps serving until no turn has been
   in a tool call for 1 s (so a model call between two tool batches does
   not end it) or the budget runs out, then shuts down as before (A then
   keeps the marker of whatever is still running). A second SIGTERM/SIGINT skips the
   rest. Needs the service manager to signal only the main process
   (systemd KillMode=mixed) and a stop timeout above the budget.

C (subagents of the dashboard run in-process and die with it, marked
"unknown" by recover_abandoned_delegations) is not re-run: their recorded
partial results now reach the parent turn via B, and the auto-continued
parent decides what to redo.

Upstream status: none of this is fixed in v0.23.0 or main (9dcf7714);
turn_marker.py, session_auto_continue.py and the reaper exit path are
unchanged there. Generated against v0.23.0 (2bd378ed), applies without
fuzz. v0.23.0 dropped the interrupt_for_session call next to
request_hard_interrupt in session_lifecycle._interrupt_session_turn; the
shutdown label rides request_hard_interrupt's tool_reason alone.

